{"capture_id":"kco-202611-0413d58730012949f54cc90945207cd49e365e786e9be583e88c319cf022911b","captured_at":"2026-11-05T22:33:23Z","event_type":"observation.captured","partition":"observations/gotcha/2026-11.jsonl","request":{"competency_facets":["CQ-VERIFY"],"contract_version":"knowledge-captured-observation.v2","destination_hint":{"path":"docs/knowledge/topics","type":"topic"},"freshness_anchor":{"digest":{"byte_length":49387,"kind":"sha256-bytes-v1","sha256":"24b4a1cd3ee1cdeb8ea6b4fbf918f7e689b03ed360bf60af0755d236f6feae39"},"path":"docs/specs/work-item-promotion-handoff/spec.md"},"kind":"gotcha","lesson":"A clause added to an acceptance criterion needs a stated driver in the spec's own Testing Strategy; a driver living only in the plan's mutable task list is invisible to the completion gate, so the criterion can pass while its clause is never tested. In a fourteen-round amendment review this surfaced three times — atomic publication, alien-lock refusal, and orphan-inertness clauses all landed in criteria and plan tasks but not in the gate-read scenarios until a reviewer caught each. When amending a criterion, walk every new clause into a Testing Strategy mode entry and scenario in the same commit.","observed_at":"2026-11-05T22:33:23Z","privacy_attestation":{"contains_instructions":false,"contains_private_data":false,"contains_secrets":false,"reviewed":true},"producer":{"workflow":"work-loop","workflow_version":"work-loop-producer-profile.v1"},"project_scope":{"audience":"project","paths":["docs/specs"]},"provenance":{"sources":[{"line_end":590,"line_start":505,"path":"docs/specs/work-item-promotion-handoff/spec.md"}]},"semantic_gate":{"artifact":"docs/specs/work-item-promotion-handoff/spec.md","name":"spec-approved"}},"request_sha256":"0413d58730012949f54cc90945207cd49e365e786e9be583e88c319cf022911b","schema_version":"observation-event.v1","state":"pending"}
{"capture_id":"kco-202611-e840df41a2a03298486d286f4422140708f88af8ea531b30ec246160c1f62a94","captured_at":"2026-11-05T22:34:57Z","event_type":"observation.captured","partition":"observations/gotcha/2026-11.jsonl","request":{"competency_facets":["CQ-DESIGN"],"contract_version":"knowledge-captured-observation.v2","destination_hint":{"path":"docs/knowledge/topics","type":"topic"},"freshness_anchor":{"digest":{"byte_length":36002,"kind":"sha256-bytes-v1","sha256":"8dedf248321aeae15487e16a59b9de2ca5d0e99c4eba4214f91e9654c5a378dc"},"path":"docs/specs/work-item-promotion-handoff/plan.md"},"kind":"gotcha","lesson":"When one task must assert a property of a surface another task edits, assign the edit and its pin to a single task whose Done-when names both. A split assignment — the asserting task running before the editing task's dependency order allows it — leaves a per-task gate that can never go green, and a cross-referenced parenthetical does not decide who owns the edit. The same review also caught the compressed cross-product sentence (two rules, two surfaces, one clause) that assigns both rules to both surfaces. State each rule against the one sentence it changes.","observed_at":"2026-11-05T22:34:57Z","privacy_attestation":{"contains_instructions":false,"contains_private_data":false,"contains_secrets":false,"reviewed":true},"producer":{"workflow":"work-loop","workflow_version":"work-loop-producer-profile.v1"},"project_scope":{"audience":"project","paths":["docs/specs"]},"provenance":{"sources":[{"line_end":670,"line_start":585,"path":"docs/specs/work-item-promotion-handoff/plan.md"}]},"semantic_gate":{"artifact":"docs/specs/work-item-promotion-handoff/plan.md","name":"plan-locked"}},"request_sha256":"e840df41a2a03298486d286f4422140708f88af8ea531b30ec246160c1f62a94","schema_version":"observation-event.v1","state":"pending"}
